// MASTER ENABLE FOR THE WHOLE RIG, despite the diag_ name. BoneBlendTick returns
// immediately without it, so no detours install, nothing is written, and the arm
// falls straight back to animation. It is NOT a logging flag - it was mistaken for
// one during a cleanup pass and switching it off silently reverted the entire arm.
//
// The name is wrong and stays for now only because renaming it would orphan the
// key in every existing settings file. Use DiagBoneBlendVerbose below for logging.
//
// [LOADBEARING: ROOT-2] diag_bone_blend_probe. NOT a probe, despite the diag_ prefix.
// [DEP: DiagBoneBlendProbe] required-for=every hook this file installs  tree=LOADBEARING
//   :25933  if (!DiagBoneBlendProbe && !ForceFlagPresent()) { ...; return; }
//   -> BoneBlendTick exits BEFORE :25943's Install(), so the writer, the player
//      tail and the final gate never exist and nothing writes a bone.
//   Registry: docs/RIG_LOADBEARING.md §2.
bool DiagBoneBlendProbe = true;   // DEFAULT ON 2026-10-01: a rig master enable (INV-1), Tier B, docs/SHIP_DEFAULTS.md
// The rig's own diagnostic logging - [WRIST], [TWISTBONE], [CLAV], the periodic
// hook report and the character table. Separate from the enable above precisely so
// the arm can run silently, which is what a timing measurement needs: LogTo holds
// a lock across file I/O per line, so leaving this on makes the logging itself one
// of the variables.
//
// >>> ⚠ THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE IS WRONG AND THE CORRECTION IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THIS
// >>> MARKER. THE ARM CANNOT "RUN SILENTLY". TURNING THIS OFF TURNS OFF THE ARM. <<<
//
// [LOADBEARING: ROOT-3] diag_bone_blend_verbose. NOT a logging switch.
// [DEP: DiagBoneBlendVerbose] required-for=engine accessor resolution  tree=LOADBEARING
//   :26542  if (!DiagBoneBlendVerbose) { return; }
//   That return sits on the 2 s report pass (the throttle is at :26520), and that
//   pass is the ONLY path that reaches :27489 ProbePlayerMatrixBuffer(), which is
//   the ONLY caller of ResolveAccessors() (:4346; grep-confirmed, the sole call
//   site is :6392). With it off, g_getWorld / g_getLocal stay NULL for the whole
//   session and SolveArmIk returns false at :6953 on every tick - SILENTLY, while
//   every arming line including "[SHOULDER] latched" still prints.
//   The kill is 942 lines away from the switch. Registry: docs/RIG_LOADBEARING.md §2.
bool DiagBoneBlendVerbose = true;   // DEFAULT ON 2026-10-01: a rig master enable (INV-1), Tier B
// THE LOGGING SWITCH THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE WANTED, 2026-10-01 - and it gates only LOG
// statements, never a path (periodic_log.h has the whole contract). Every timer-driven
// report line that ran with no probe on goes through LOG_PERIODIC and is silent while
// this is off. The report pass itself still runs, so ROOT-3's accessor resolution is
// untouched. Shipped OFF (Jay 2026-10-01, cleanup option A).
// [DEP: DiagPeriodicLogs] required-for=diag_periodic_logs, the timer-driven report lines (LOG_PERIODIC)  tree=PROBE-ONLY
#if !defined(DLVR_BETA_LEAN)   // BETA lean: constexpr false in periodic_log.h / boneblend.h (docs/BETA_CENSUS.md)
bool DiagPeriodicLogs     = false;
bool DiagWorldWriter      = false;
#endif
// Read-only: the anti-wall deformation state, the weapon category it is gated on,
// and which callers construct projectiles. Writes nothing. See the antiwall
// namespace near the bottom of this file for the full provenance.
#if !defined(DLVR_BETA_LEAN)   // BETA lean: constexpr false in boneblend.h (docs/BETA_CENSUS.md)
bool AntiWallProbe        = false;
#endif
// Re-derive the IK anchor from the shoulder when the eye bone goes anatomically
// implausible. This is the bow's broken right arm: measured |eye - shoulder| of
// at least 0.99 m on a rig whose entire arm is 0.546 m, which made the solver aim
// 1.35 m from the shoulder and the reach limiter eat 0.7-0.8 m of it. See the
// ANCHOR GUARD block in SolveArmIk.
// Take the arm bones off the animation system with Block(i), so the game's own
// pose cannot compete with ours. Measured with a bow held: an animation pose
// sitting ~90 deg off ours, rewriting our locals, while our own elbow pole and
// plane normal are provably clean - so the fight is the problem, not our maths.
// Write the solved bones at the player-update TAIL (gamedll+0xBFC170), strictly
// later in the frame than anything else we do, instead of disabling things
// earlier - four attempts at disabling failed.
bool  RigWriteAtPlayerTail = true;   // DEFAULT ON 2026-10-01 (Tier B)
bool  RigBlockArmBones    = false;
// Block(i) also flips bit 0x2 on the bone's mesh element, which is a RENDER bit -
// it made both arms vanish. This clears it back, leaving the animation block in
// place. Separate setting so that if the arms return and the animation also
// resumes fighting us, we learn that bit 0x2 was load-bearing for the block.
bool  RigBlockKeepVisible = true;
// (rig_anchor_guard - the anchor substitution below the latch: REMOVED 2026-10-07, audit section 2.)
// THE BOW RUNAWAY FIX. The solve took its shoulder S straight out of element 48's
// LIVE world matrix, and our own write is what moves element 48 - so S fed back into
// the rotation that displaced it. Measured, rig ON vs OFF, same binary: with the rig
// off element 48's local translation sits at its rest 0.1718 through the whole bow
// window; with the rig on it RAMPS 0.17 -> 0.59 -> 0.81 -> 1.05 -> 1.55 -> 1.60, in
// exact lockstep with |clav-upper| to four decimals. Monotonic growth out of an
// orthonormal (bounded) write is positional feedback and nothing else.
//
// So take S from bones our write cannot reach: the clavicle, measured clean in BOTH
// runs (|hspine1-clav| exactly 0.0853 every sample, basis columns exactly 1.0000),
// composed with element 48's authored rest offset latched while it is still sane.
bool  RigStableShoulder   = true;   // DEFAULT ON 2026-10-01 (Tier B)
// true = freeze the latched offset outright (the version confirmed working in
// the headset). false = keep the live direction and clamp only the length.
bool  RigShoulderFreezeDir = true;
#if !defined(DLVR_BETA_LEAN)   // BETA lean: constexpr false in boneblend.h (docs/BETA_CENSUS.md)
bool  DiagBodyYaw          = false;
#endif
// [SEGLEN]. Read-only. Is the bicep-to-elbow segment changing LENGTH? Element
// 49's local translation IS that length; nothing clamps it, and we never write
// it. See the probe itself for the whole argument.
#if !defined(DLVR_BETA_LEAN)   // BETA lean: constexpr false in boneblend.h (docs/BETA_CENSUS.md)
bool  DiagSegmentLen       = false;
#endif
// [STRAFE]. Read-only. Does lateral movement lean the body, moving the shoulder
// under a target that is anchored to the EYE? Measures the eye->shoulder VECTOR
// in the body frame - the length is already logged and is blind to a lean.
#if !defined(DLVR_BETA_LEAN)   // BETA lean: constexpr false in boneblend.h (docs/BETA_CENSUS.md)
bool  DiagStrafe           = false;
#endif
// [YAWSTRAFE]. Read-only. Does STRAFING move bodyYaw with no stick input?
// [SWAY] cannot answer it - that probe marks turn windows by CAMERA rate, so a
// strafe with no stick reads as "STILL" and is discarded.
#if !defined(DLVR_BETA_LEAN)   // BETA lean: constexpr false in boneblend.h (docs/BETA_CENSUS.md)
bool  DiagYawStrafe        = false;
#endif
// [YAWNOISE]. Read-only. The per-tick step of the RAW camera yaw, split by
// whether anything is moving. The STILL floor IS the noise - and every filter
// this project has built was sized against an amplitude nobody had measured.
#if !defined(DLVR_BETA_LEAN)   // BETA lean: constexpr false in boneblend.h (docs/BETA_CENSUS.md)
bool  DiagYawNoise         = false;
#endif
// Undo the engine IK's bone stretch at the player tail. See ClampBoneLength.
bool  RigSuppressStretch  = false;
bool  DiagFastIkGlobals   = false;
// (rig_fastik_blend / rig_fastik_maxdelta: REMOVED 2026-10-07, audit section 2 - never applied: the write hit read-only memory.)
// (rig_fastik_hook - the player-only maxDelta bound's switch: REMOVED 2026-10-07 with the bound itself.)
bool  RigFinalGateHooks   = true;   // DEFAULT ON 2026-10-01 (Tier B)
bool  RigWriteAtFinalGate = true;   // DEFAULT ON 2026-10-01 (Tier B)
// RESTORE ELEMENT 48'S AUTHORED BONE LENGTH AT THE PLAYER-UPDATE TAIL.
//
// Measured, run 20:43:39: we hand the engine element 48 at exactly its authored
// 0.1718 (|A| = 0.1718 in EVERY sample with nothing equipped and with a melee
// weapon) and the engine hands back a mean 0.6575 with the bow, peak 1.4526 -
// including 22 consecutive frames with both controllers ON THE FLOOR. So the
// write is the engine's, it is bow-only, and it is not driven by our input or
// the player's.
//
// LENGTH ONLY, DIRECTION LEFT ALONE - the invariant this project established the
// hard way. A bone's length cannot change by rotation, so the length is the
// provably corrupt part; the direction is legitimately animated, and the last
// attempt that froze the whole vector pinned the shoulder to its melee pose and
// the user saw the arm start off to the LEFT. Take the engine's live direction,
// impose the latched authored length.
//
// NO SANITY BAND. The previous tail undo (rig_suppress_stretch) refused to act
// above 0.60 m and so stopped dead at +0.4274 while the bone reached 1.55 - it
// skipped precisely the excursions worth catching. This acts on every frame.
bool  RigRestoreE48Len    = true;
// The LEFT upper arm's length clamp - element 12, the mirror of the above.
// Measured 2026-09-10: elem12's local |t| ranges 0.1081-0.2419 (spread 0.1338 m)
// against a latched rest of 0.1717, while elem48 holds 0.1718 flat. Its own
// setting rather than sharing RigRestoreE48Len, so a left-side regression can be
// switched off without disarming the right arm's five-month fix.
bool  RigRestoreLeftLen   = true;
// [DEP: rig_restore_upper_dir] required-for=the left-arm flicker with a firearm and the pistol/shotgun swap jump (INV-330;
//      Jay 2026-10-01: "Build the fix.") - the game moves the upper arm's local translation DIRECTION (slot 407 C81190
//      steadily on the left, a second unidentified writer for < 1 s at a draw on both arms: 11-13 deg = 33-39 mm at the
//      shoulder) and the two restores above put back its LENGTH only, while the solve hangs S off the AUTHORED direction
//      (rig_shoulder_freeze_dir). With this on, all four restore sites write the whole latched vector g_upperRestLocal
//      instead - the drawn shoulder is the one the solve used. Acts only where the length restore acts (needs
//      rig_restore_e48_len / rig_restore_left_len and the [SHOULDER] latch). (RigRestoreUpperDir) tree=INERT.
//      DEFAULT ON since 2026-10-01 (Jay, after run C: no flicker, no pistol/shotgun jump, bow correct - "Set the default
//      to 1"). Needs the rig solving (the latch is made in SolveArmIk - controller poses) - HEADSET.
bool  RigRestoreUpperDir  = true;
// [DEP: rig_elbow_right_axis_mirror] required-for=the RIGHT elbow coming toward the chest (the elbow inversion, Jay
//      2026-10-02; INV-333: the pole's hand term carries the elbow inside; INV-336: the right baked grip constant's
//      hand axis is 19.8-41.2 deg from the measured right arm, the mirror of the LEFT constant's 2.3-21.8 deg).
//      With this on, the RIGHT arm's elbow-pole hand term reads cWorld . mirror(kGripBaked[LEFT] column 0) instead
//      of the right constant's column 0. ONLY the pole: `grip`, the wrist step-back, the drawn hand, the weapon and
//      the shot are untouched (INV-336 addendum, INV-337). The left arm is untouched. (RigElbowRightAxisMirror)
//      tree=INERT. DEFAULT OFF until Jay verifies it. Needs the rig solving with a hand axis (controller poses,
//      rig_wrist=1) and rig_elbow_follow_hand > 0 - HEADSET.
bool  RigElbowRightAxisMirror = false;
// ===== ARM STAGE 1 - docs/SPEC-ARM-HIERARCHY-2026-10-02.md §2 (Jay 2026-10-02: "Start the arm work, stage 1"). =====
// [DEP: rig_grip_right_fix] required-for=[GRIPFIX] + the RIGHT hand's orientation, wrist target and elbow (INV-336: the
//      right baked grip constant's hand axis is 19.8-41.2 deg off the measured right arm; INV-343 replay: correcting it
//      takes the right pec elbow -58 -> +52 mm and the sight-to-eye elbow -129 -> +23 mm with today's elbow rule).
//      (RigGripRightFix) tree=LOADBEARING when > 0. DEFAULT 0. 0..1 = the fraction of the rotation (controller grip
//      frame, 28.4 deg at 1) carrying kGripBaked[RIGHT] column 0 onto the mirror of kGripBaked[LEFT] column 0; the WHOLE
//      right matrix is carried by it. Applied to the grip the solve READS (hand axis + wrist step-back, and the drawn
//      hand); the stored constant is untouched. RIGHT arm only, only while rig_grip_use_baked=1. Read every solve - live.
//      Needs the rig solving with a grip (controller poses, rig_wrist=1) - HEADSET.
float RigGripRightFix = 0.0f;
// [DEP: rig_grip_right_fix_weapon] required-for=where the RIGHT-hand weapon points while rig_grip_right_fix > 0 (the
//      weapon rides the drawn hand, INV-336 addendum; Last Wish's barrel sits ~14.5 deg outward of the arm today,
//      INV-337 addendum 2). (RigGripRightFixWeapon) tree=LOADBEARING while rig_grip_right_fix > 0 and this < 1.
//      DEFAULT 1 = the weapon turns with the hand (stays seated in the palm). 0 = the weapon keeps today's direction
//      against the controller (ItemCtlPlace's rel path counter-rotates the drawn basis about the wrist by the part not
//      followed). Needs item_place_rel=1 and a held item. Read every placement - live.
float RigGripRightFixWeapon = 1.0f;
// ===== THE BODY-AXIS ELBOW POLE - docs/SPEC-ELBOW-POLE-2026-10-02.md §4c, INV-339 (Jay 2026-10-02: "Build it"). =====
// [DEP: rig_elbow_pole_mode] required-for=the elbow inversion on BOTH arms (INV-333: the hand term carries the elbow
//      inside the shoulder-wrist line; D-169: no hand axis fixes it). 0 = today's pole (world-down blended with the
//      hand axis), untouched. 1 = pole = dir x A (right) / A x dir (left), A = a body-yaw-frame axis tilted off the
//      arm's own side: perpendicular to the arm by construction, the same function for both arms, hand POSITION
//      only. In mode 1 rig_elbow_follow_hand, rig_left_elbow_follow_hand, rig_elbow_pole_max_up and
//      rig_elbow_right_axis_mirror do not act. (RigElbowPoleMode) tree=INERT at 0. Was DEFAULT 0 until Jay verifies it.
//      Needs the rig solving (controller poses) - HEADSET.
//      SHIPPED 1 since 2026-10-02 (Jay, INV-354: "That's significantly better ... that's fine. Good work.") - with
//      rig_elbow_wrist_relief=0 and rig_wrist_overflow_deg=60, his disk.
//      MODE 2 (2026-10-25, docs/SPEC-ELBOW-REAL-ARM-2026-10-25.md, Jay: "Yes" / "Native"): mode 1's pole, then the REAL-ARM
//      correction (elbowreal_logic.h SolveSwivel) - the swivel moves only as far as the player's real wrist (vs the calibrated
//      forearm-in-hand) and then the forearm roll need, ranked. Needs arm_fit.txt *_forearm_in_hand; without it = mode 1, logged.
//      Faded out for the pinned two-handed left arm, the left bow arm and a short arm. tree=INERT below 2. HEADSET.
//      Mode 2 is Tier C (not verified in the headset), SHIPPED 2 on Jay's call for the 2026-10-27 tester package ("Mode 2", asked
//      against "Mode 1 (Recommended)"); = his disk. Without arm_fit.txt's *_forearm_in_hand it runs as mode 1, logged.
int   RigElbowPoleMode    = 2;
// [DEP: rig_elbow_axis_fwd] required-for=rig_elbow_pole_mode=1 - the FORWARD part of A (OUT is 1). 0.30 from the
//      replay (INV-339). Moves the hanging arm's elbow outward and the singular axis off straight-sideways.
//      (RigElbowAxisFwd) tree=INERT while the mode is 0.
float RigElbowAxisFwd     = 0.30f;
// [DEP: rig_elbow_axis_up] required-for=rig_elbow_pole_mode=1 - the UP part of A. 0.17 from the replay (INV-339): the
//      forward-reaching arm's elbow goes down and slightly out, and the pec hold's elbow does not go forward.
//      (RigElbowAxisUp) tree=INERT while the mode is 0.
float RigElbowAxisUp      = 0.17f;
// [DEP: rig_elbow_axis_fwd_side] required-for=rig_elbow_pole_mode=1 with the arm OUT TO ITS OWN SIDE (INV-356: for an arm
//      along OUT the pole is OUT x (fwd F + up U) = fwd UP - up FORWARD, so the shipped +0.30 puts the elbow 50-94 mm ABOVE
//      the arm with -147 / -174 deg of forearm twist - Jay 2026-10-02: "twisted when out to the sides"). The FORWARD part of
//      A is blended from rig_elbow_axis_fwd to THIS value as the arm direction's OUT component goes 0.55 -> 0.85; a
//      negative value leans the axis BACK there, which puts the elbow under the arm (replay, -0.60: 57-97 mm below,
//      |twist| median 3-43 deg) and moves the singular direction 30 deg behind straight-out. (RigElbowAxisFwdSide)
//      tree=INERT while it equals rig_elbow_axis_fwd or the mode is 0. Was DEFAULT 0.30 = no change until Jay verifies.
//      Read every solve - live.
//      SHIPPED -0.60 since 2026-10-02 = Jay's disk (INV-358: side elbow 70-87 mm below the arm; Jay: "They looked right
//      to the sides, albeit a slight bend at the elbows ... more than passable at this stage" and "Elbows are fine").
//      tree=LOADBEARING for the pole in mode 1 at this value.
float RigElbowAxisFwdSide = -0.60f;
// [DEP: rig_elbow_hand_weight] required-for=rig_elbow_pole_mode=1 - WRIST RELIEF: how much of the old hand term is
//      blended back in (both unit and perpendicular to the arm, so |pole| >= 1 - 2w). ONE weight for both arms,
//      hard-capped at 0.30. 0 = the wrist never moves the elbow (CPVR's choice; D-169). (RigElbowHandWeight)
//      tree=INERT while the mode is 0.
float RigElbowHandWeight  = 0.0f;
// [DEP: rig_elbow_short_hold_cm] required-for=rig_elbow_pole_mode=1 - THE SHORT-ARM HOLD (Jay 2026-10-13: "Do it"; INV-555 add. 2-3).
//      With the wrist target within this many cm of S the arm direction dir = (T - S)/d is the direction of a few-cm vector -
//      tracking noise - and the pole dir x A (and so the elbow, a whole upper arm from S) followed it: up to 0.29 m ABOVE S in the
//      bow mimic / right fold holds; right-hand d < 15 cm on 2.4-11.3% of samples in play, 62% of them bow draws in relax_run1.
//      With this > 0: the pole's confidence also fades with d - full below half this distance, smoothstep up to it - so the pole
//      HOLDS its last direction (the existing memory, 0.25 s expiry) as the hand comes in. Only when a previous pole exists.
//      0 = off (bit-identical). tree=INERT at 0 / LOADBEARING on (the pole for short arms). HEADSET. Was DEFAULT 0 - Tier C.
//      v2 (2026-10-13, Jay: "Build the upper-arm hold"; INV-556 - the pole hold alone left 15 jumps of 30-127 deg because the
//      elbow sits ~12 cm ALONG the noisy dir): the same weight also holds the UPPER ARM's direction; the forearm aims from that
