// bone axis - so the scalar-along-X form is wrong in DIRECTION as well as length.
// Was default OFF.
bool  RigWristBackFromAsset  = true;    // DEFAULT ON 2026-10-01 (Tier B)
// THE LEFT ARM. Was off by default so the working right arm cannot regress behind it.
bool  RigLeftArm             = true;    // DEFAULT ON 2026-10-01 (Tier B)
// The two things that genuinely do NOT mirror, kept as settings because guessing
// has been expensive today:
//
//  - The grip roll. The right measured +90; the left is probably -90 by symmetry,
//    but "probably" is what cost several runs.
//  - Deviation SIDES. Ulnar is toward the pinky and radial toward the thumb, so on
//    the left hand they sit on the opposite side in bone space and the sign pairing
//    flips. Get it wrong and the left wrist gets radial's tight 18 degrees where it
//    needs ulnar's 38 - exactly the clipping just fixed on the right.
float RigLeftGripRollDeg     = 0.0f;    // DEFAULT 0 2026-10-01 (Jay's value, Tier B);
bool  RigLeftMirrorDeviation = true;
bool  RigLeftWristSwapAxes   = true;
// THE HAND BONE DOES NOT SHARE THE FOREARM'S AXIS CONVENTION.
//
// The grip is captured as "hand aligned with the forearm", which forces the
// hand's local matrix to identity - and that quietly assumes the two bones use
// the same roll convention. Biped rigs routinely do not: measured in the headset,
// this one sits a quarter turn out, so a straight forearm left the palm facing
// left. Identity was never the rig's rest pose, only the most obvious guess at
// it, and this is the measured correction rather than a fudge.
float RigGripRollDeg         = 0.0f;    // DEFAULT 0 2026-10-01 (Jay's value, Tier B);
// The other two axes of the hand correction. We carried one angle, which cannot express a
// correction needing the other axes. Default 0 so they are exact no-ops until
// tuned, leaving rig_grip_roll_deg's meaning unchanged.
float RigGripPitchDeg        = 0.0f;
float RigGripYawDeg          = 0.0f;
float RigLeftGripPitchDeg    = 0.0f;
float RigLeftGripYawDeg      = 0.0f;
// r_handholder (71), the weapon socket, sits this far from r_hand (53) in the
// rig - so it is the real wrist-to-grip distance, not an estimate.
float RigHandLen             = 0.0970f;
// NOT the shoulder mover, despite the name reading that way. We only ever write
// bone ROTATIONS - the translation column stays the engine's - so this moves the
// solve's idea of where the arm is rooted, i.e. where the upper arm AIMS. The
// visible shoulder does not move. Use the protraction below for that.
float RigShoulderForward     = 0.0f;
// CLAVICLE PROTRACTION - the real lever for a shoulder sitting too far back.
//
// A clavicle rotating the shoulder forward is protraction, an actual joint motion
// with an actual range (roughly 15-20 degrees). Distinct from RigClavicleShare,
// which swings the clavicle TOWARD THE HAND and caused the shoulder to read as
// shoved forward and misaligned when it was doing the work alone; a fixed
// protraction is a resting posture, not a reach.
float RigClavicleProtractDeg = 0.0f;
// How far the elbow is allowed to swivel to line the forearm up with the hand.
// 0 reproduces the old fixed world-down pole exactly, which makes it the A/B
// against the [WRIST] angles rather than something to be taken on trust.
float RigElbowFollowHand     = 0.70f;
// THE LEFT ARM'S ELBOW-POLE WEIGHT, and it is BELOW 0.5 BY PROOF, not by taste.
//
// The pole blends world-down against the hand term:
//     pole_y = (1-w)*(-1) + w*(-hp_y/hpl)
// The cliff is pole_y crossing zero, which needs  w*(-hp_y/hpl) > (1-w).
// |hp_y/hpl| <= 1 by construction, so the left term cannot exceed w, and the
// crossing therefore requires  w > 1-w,  i.e.  w > 0.5.
//
//     ==> AT w <= 0.5 THE CLIFF IS MATHEMATICALLY IMPOSSIBLE. <==
//
// AND THE max_up CLAMP DOES NOT SAVE IT - measured. With w=0.70 the LEFT clamps
// 236-280 frames per window AND STILL FLIPS 3 TIMES, while the right clamps 0
// and flips 0. Clamping pole[1] to 0 stops the VERTICAL crossing and creates a
// SECOND degeneracy in its place: a pole pinned flat in the horizontal plane can
// swing through parallel-to-dir, and `axis = dir x pole` flips there just the
// same. That is why the guard fires constantly on the left and the arm still
// rolls - the archive's "a cheap anatomical guard, not as a fix" was right.
// Holding w under 0.5 means the clamp never engages, so NEITHER degeneracy is
// reachable.
//
// 0.45, not 0.50: at exactly 0.5 the crossing is marginal (pole_y can reach 0),
// and a value that only just works is not a guard.
//
// PER-SIDE because the RIGHT measures clean at 0.70 and is described as "just
// about perfect" - there is no reason to move it, and every reason not to touch
// a working arm while fixing a broken one.
//
// COST, stated plainly: the elbow follows the hand less on the left, so its
// placement is a little less natural than the right's. That is the trade for
// removing a 180 degree arm roll, and it is adjustable.
float RigLeftElbowFollowHand = 0.45f;
// THE RIG'S ARM IS SHORTER THAN A REAL ONE - 0.2865 + 0.2649 = 0.5514 m of reach,
// measured off the rig, so the character simply has a ~55 cm arm. The mesh cannot
// be lengthened (we write rotations only; bone lengths are the engine's) and
// lying about the lengths in the solve would make the hand miss its target rather
// than reach further. So the mapping is where this gets handled.
//
// Scale, about the SHOULDER rather than the head, so it changes reach without
// swinging where the hand sits angularly. 1 is untouched 1:1.
// SHIPPED 0.9 since 2026-10-02 = Jay's disk, HIS call as a stopgap for the reach shortfall (INV-361 / INV-363: "Leave it
// as .9 for me for the moment, shipped too"). ⚠ It is a body-dependent value (SPEC-ARM-FIT §5) and it needs
// rig_forearm_aim_achieved=1, or the weapon leaves the hand by ~2.5 cm (INV-362).
float RigReachScale          = 0.9f;
// Where the reach starts easing off, as a fraction of maximum. Below it, motion is
// exactly 1:1; above it the last stretch compresses smoothly and asymptotes
// instead of hitting a wall, so full extension is approached rather than struck.
// 1 restores the old hard clamp.
float RigReachSoft           = 0.85f;
// [DEP: rig_reach_margin_mm] required-for=the arm STRAIGHTENING at full reach (INV-360: Jay 2026-10-02: "I'd hit full
//      extension before my arms did"). The reach cap is the bone sum (0.5514 m) minus this margin; at the cap the
//      elbow still sits sqrt-of-the-margin off the shoulder-wrist line: 5 mm -> 37.0 mm, 0.5 mm -> 11.7 mm, 0.1 mm ->
//      5.2 mm. Today's runs ask a median 0.62-0.67 m of a 0.5514 m arm, so the arm sits AT the cap whenever Jay's is
//      extended and that 37 mm (41-49 measured, with the easing) is the bend he sees. (RigReachMarginMm)
//      tree=LOADBEARING (it is the cap in S-20; every arm, every mode). DEFAULT 5 = the constant it replaces.
//      Read every solve - live.
float RigReachMarginMm       = 5.0f;
// [DEP: rig_forearm_aim_achieved] required-for=the DRAWN hand sitting where the solve says it is - and so the weapon
//      sitting in it - whenever the used reach is shorter than the asked one (INV-362). The solve aimed the forearm at
//      the asked target T; with rig_reach_scale 0.9 that drew the hand 22-27 mm (median) from the achieved wrist the
//      weapon is placed at (Jay 2026-10-02: "Weapons were slightly off palm/clipping through fingers/hand"), and
//      10-16 mm in the shipped easing zone at long reach. On: the forearm aims at S + dir . dc. Identical while
//      dc == d (scale 1, inside the knee). (RigForearmAimAchieved) tree=INERT at 0. Was DEFAULT 0 until Jay verifies.
//      (The mode-1 relief's swing prediction, which aimed at T, was removed 2026-10-07.) Read every solve - live.
//      SHIPPED ON since 2026-10-02 (INV-363: forearm end 0.0 mm off the achieved wrist in 22 rows, weapon 4.6-7.8 mm
//      from the drawn hand at reach scale 0.9; Jay, asked whether the machete / Last Wish / Volkatronix sat where they
//      normally do: "Yes they did."). tree=LOADBEARING: rig_reach_scale=0.9 ships with it.
bool  RigForearmAimAchieved  = true;
// Of the pronation the forearm delivers, how much is routed to the rig's own
// r_foretwist bone (element 50) instead of the forearm bone itself.
//
// To us the forearm is ONE rigid bone, so rolling it turns the elbow end by
// exactly as much as the wrist end. A real forearm does not do that - pronation
// is zero at the elbow and full at the wrist, which is why turning your palm over
// does not rotate your elbow. The rig knows this and carries r_foretwist at the
// far end for the purpose; the mesh weighted between the two twists gradually
// along the limb. We were bypassing it.
//
// 1 is the anatomical routing. 0 is the old behaviour, i.e. the A/B.
float RigTwistToTwistBone    = 0.0f;
// ===== THE FOREARM TWIST CHAIN - INV-355 (Jay 2026-10-02: "visible twist/candywrapping in the elbows"). =====
// [DEP: rig_twist_chain] required-for=the forearm twist being drawn ALONG the forearm instead of at the elbow. The arm
//      mesh is weighted elbow -> wrist to forearm (49/13), foretwist1 (51/15), foretwist (50/14) + foretwistt (52/16),
//      hand (INV-355, read from player_6_fpp's skin weights). With rig_forearm_twist=1 the whole twist is a roll of bone
//      49, so the only skin that takes it up is the ~10 cm at the elbow where upperarm blends into forearm. This is the
//      FRACTION of that roll taken OFF bone 49: the hand's own local carries it instead (the existing residual), the
//      foretwist pin is written rolled by the same amount about the forearm's axis (so the wrist-end skin and the watch
//      turn with the hand), and the foretwist1 pin keeps rig_twist_chain_mid of it. NOT rig_twist_to_twistbone (D-7):
//      that rolled 50 alone, left its child 51 turning the whole amount, and its driven slot is dropped when the
//      clavicle hold fills the fourth slot. The writes go through the [SUBTREE] pins, which already own 50/51/14/15.
//      (RigTwistChain) tree=INERT at 0. Was DEFAULT 0 until Jay verifies. Needs rig_subtree_rest=1, rig_pin_foretwist=1,
//      rig_twist_to_twistbone=0 and both pins latched - else it REFUSES by name and bone 49 keeps the roll. Read every
//      solve - live.
//      SHIPPED 1.0 since 2026-10-02 = Jay's disk (INV-359: "It looks much more acceptable now. The value I set is what it
//      should stay and ship with"). tree=LOADBEARING for the forearm's skin at this value.
float RigTwistChain          = 1.0f;
// [DEP: rig_twist_chain_mid] required-for=rig_twist_chain - the share of the wrist's twist the MID-forearm bone
//      (foretwist1, 51/15, 0.106 m up the forearm from the wrist) shows. It is a CHILD of foretwist, so its pin is
//      counter-rolled by (1 - this). 0.5 = the forearm skin turns half way at its middle. (RigTwistChainMid)
//      tree=INERT while rig_twist_chain is 0.
float RigTwistChainMid       = 0.5f;
// [DEP: rig_twist_chain_max_deg] required-for=rig_twist_chain not showing more forearm twist than an arm can make
//      (INV-358: with the chain at 1 the forearm showed everything the solve asked, up to ~100 deg; Jay: "still a little
//      bit too much twist in the forearms than what is naturally possible" and "build the cap ... as natural as
//      possible"). The forearm's shown twist is linear to 75% of this and eased into it; the excess stays on bone 49.
//      (RigTwistChainMaxDeg) tree=INERT while rig_twist_chain is 0 or this is 180 (no cap). DEFAULT 180 until Jay
//      verifies a value. Read every solve - live.
//      SHIPPED 71.5 since 2026-10-02 = the value Jay set in the headset (INV-359; shown twist never past 71.5 deg in
//      5,175 samples, hand 0.0-0.5 deg off the command). tree=LOADBEARING with the chain on.
float RigTwistChainMaxDeg    = 71.5f;
// The rig's own measured segment lengths (a child bone's local offset IS its
// parent's length). Exposed because a wrong length shows up as a limb that
// looks the wrong proportion long before it shows up as a reach failure.
float RigUpperArmLen         = 0.2865f;
float RigForearmLen          = 0.2649f;
float RigClavicleShare       = 0.0f;
